<section xml:id="syllabus" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Syllabus: Differential Equations with Linear Algebra</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is the syllabus outlining expectations, course components, and detailing the grading system for the class.  See the Parent Night handout for more general information
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- Include chapters -->
  <subsection xml:id="sec-behavior">
    <title>Behavior Expectations</title>
      
<paragraphs>
  <title>Responsibility</title>
	<p><ul>
		<li>listening: with mind and heart to whoever has the floor</li>
		<li>thinking: about what we read, see, and hear means, how it connects with ideas and information we already hold and what implications it has</li>
		<li>boundaries: respecting state and federal laws, school rules, classroom and assignment policies, and individual personal boundaries</li>
	</ul></p>	
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Accountability</title>
	<p>for acquiring knowledge and skills</p>
		<p><ul>
			<li>group work during class</li>
			<li>problem sets</li>
				<!--<li>lab reports</li>-->
			<li>quizzes, tests, and exams</li>
		</ul></p>		
	<p>for violation of boundaries</p>
		<p><ul>
			<li>one-on-one conversation</li>
			<li>emails to parents or advisor</li>
			<li>referral to Dean's office</li>
		</ul></p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Ethics</title>
	<p>The success of the scientific enterprise rests upon two ethical pillars.  The first of them is the obligation to tell the truth, which includes avoiding fabrication, falsification, and plagiarism.  The second is the obligation to treat people well, which encourages fair and respectful relationships and eschews bias, whether implicit or explicit. <fn>Adapted from <url href="https://ori.hhs.gov/definition-research-misconduct" visual="https://ori.hhs.gov/definition-research-misconduct">Office of Research Integrity research misconduct definitions</url> as accessed 8/20/24} and  <url href="https://www.aps.org/about/governance/policies-procedures/ethics" visual="https://www.aps.org/about/governance/policies-procedures/ethics">American Physical Society guidelines on ethics</url> accessed 8/20/24.</fn>
	</p>
	<p><ul>
		<li>Fabrication is making up data or results and recording or reporting them.</li>
		<li>Falsification is manipulating research materials, equipment, or processes or changing or omitting data or results such that the research is not accurately represented in the research record.</li>
		<li>Plagiarism is the appropriation without appropriate credit of ideas, processes, results, or words that did not come from the assigned authors of the work.  It does not matter whether the source is human or AI; dead or alive.</li>
	</ul></p>

	
</paragraphs>
<paragraphs>
  <title>AI / Machine Learning / Whole Language Models / Generative AI</title>
	<p>Machine Learning encompasses a broad category of tools.  As with any tool, they can be used productively or destructively.  For this class, you may use generative AI as a resource for producing more practice problems.  You may NOT use it to write assignments.  You may NOT enter assignments or parts of assignments from me into an AI to ask for similar questions. </p> 
	
	<p>You are welcome to use spell-checking and grammar-checking on typewritten responses for any assignment in my class.  Please do not use tools that guide you in changing the tone or structure of what you write.  Auto-completion in spreadsheets is fine - but make sure it autocompletes what you actually meant and not something else. </p>
	
	
</paragraphs>
<paragraphs>
  <title>Classroom Policies</title>
	<p><ul>
		<li>Time in class should be focussed on the class.  Think, listen, and learn.</li>
		<li>School iPads (personal iPads are not allowed) and laptops should be in use for class activities or put away.  Do not take photos/videos or make sound recordings of people without explicit permission from all the people.</li>
		<li>This classroom is a shared space.  It needs to be picked up and cleaned up routinely.  If you got it out, put it away.  If you made a mess, clean it up.</li>
		<li>Discussions with your current classmates of problems sets and labs are always encouraged with the constraint that submitted work must reflect your own final understanding of the concepts and information.  </li>
		<li>Discussions of anything relevant to the class with Dr. Horner are always encouraged.  Advisors and Learning Support are also good non-content-specific resources.</li>
		<li>Unless instructions say otherwise, use of AI tools to complete assignments is forbidden.</li>
		<li>Discussions of quizzes/tests/exams with classmates is forbidden until the quiz/test/exam is graded and returned.</li>
		<li>As an honors/AP/post-AP course, content-specific tutoring is allowed only under extraordinary circumstances.  Learning Support is fine.</li>
		<li>If you must leave the classroom during class, make sure Dr. Horner knows you are leaving and on what errand (bathroom, nurse, early dismissal, <etc/>)</li>
	</ul></p>	
</paragraphs>
</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="sec-activities">
<title>Activities</title>
<paragraphs>  
<title>New Material</title>
		<p>Expect to take notes every day in class.  This is a way of processing information, of storing it (both electronically and in your brain), and keeping track of questions you have.  You may use paper or your school iPad but set up a way to keep your notes organized.  For most people, the process of taking notes is itself helpful in remembering and thinking about the material.  In addition, you should plan on reviewing and annotating your notes outside of class to consolidate your understanding.  You should also have your in-progress problem set available during class because you may generally ask a few questions at some point during class. </p>
		
		<p>Expect to work in small groups for at least part of each class session.  The goal is to increase your understanding of the material by working on new concepts immediately and through discussion with peers. Normally, this is checked by conversation with peers and on-the-spot overview by Dr. Horner.  Occasionally, a group submission may be required in which case the points will go in the Practice category.</p>
</paragraphs>	

<paragraphs>
<title>Lab: 16% of semester grade</title>
		<p>Collecting data will always be a group activity.  In some cases, your group will turn in one copy of the data.  In other cases, you'll each need to turn in a copy with further work.  I expect that the data will be the same for all members of the group.  Data is graded very lightly for correctness (did you collect the correct kinds of data and is it accurate within the usual range for this type of lab) and strictly for accurate labels including accurate units.</p>
		
		<p>Copying data from other groups is permissible only in dire circumstances and with <em>advance</em> permission from Dr. Horner; otherwise, it is academic dishonesty.  Falsifying, changing, or making up your data is always academic dishonesty.</p>
		
		<p>Calculations and graphs may get done during class or may need to be done afterward depending on timing.  In either case, all members of the group must contribute meaningfully to the process.</p>
		
		<p>The discussion will almost always be a single sentence in a prescribed structure given in the lab instructions.  This is where your group concisely and in your own words articulates the <em>claim(s)</em>, <em>evidence</em>, and <em>reasoning</em> that extracts meaning from your raw data. Again, all members of the group must contribute meaningfully to the process.</p>
		
		<p>Asking Dr. Horner and classmates for advice is fine.  Copying from any source, including AI, is academic dishonesty.</p>
	
		<p>Everyone in the group needs to assign themselves to a Canvas lab group so that one submission works for all and feedback goes to everyone.</p>    
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>		
	<title>Materials</title>
		<p>The daily notes slides and homework assignments will generally tell you what sections of our textbook are relevant and may also point you towards practice sites, videos, or simulations.  These are resources to help you understand the material better and be more comfortable with it.  If you miss class, start with these resources and then meet with Dr. Horner as necessary so you understand the material.</p>
		
		<p>We will be using  <pubtitle>Differential Equations &amp; Linear Algebra</pubtitle> by Edwards, Penney, and Calvis.  Additional, on-line, resources are listed in the Canvas document <articletitle>Resources for the whole year</articletitle>.</p>
		
