		<p>You may use any scientific calculator in class.  The TI-84 (or the TI-89) work well.  You may also use a calculator app on your iPad <em>except</em> during tests.</p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>		
	<title>Problem Sets: 12% of semester grade</title>
		<p>There are two goals here: one is for you to practice so that ideas and skills will stick better; the other is for you to check your level of understanding so you know whether or not you need to bring questions to Extra Help / Office Hours or a free bell.</p>
		
		<p>You may talk to classmates, your teacher, friends, and family, but not AI unless otherwise instructed in the assignment, <em>about</em> answering these questions but what you turn in needs to be your own understanding of them (with or without the conversations).  Copying from <em>any</em> source is academic dishonesty.</p>
		
		<p>Problem sets are typically posted in two versions: an interactive version and a pdf version suitable for writing on directly using your iPad.  Answers will appear in the interactive version with a click.  Please use the provided answers to check your work. Please upload a single pdf of your work to Canvas for submission.</p>
		
		<p>Problem sets are typically posted at the beginning of a unit and due approximate one or two weeks later, often the day of the test over that material.</p>
			
			<p>There are required problems, in sections with <q>Set</q> in the title for 93%, and <q>Pick</q> problems labeled as either 1% or 0.5% from which up to a total of an additional 7% can be completed.</p>
			
	  <p>Problem sets are due at the <alert>beginning</alert> of class on the date posted in Canvas.  Assignments submitted later that school day (by 3:45pm) will lose 5%; later homework will lose 10% per school day (capped at 50% loss).  Homework will be graded on completion where completion means you explain clearly how you arrived at your answer.  This might be in words, drawings/graphs, or equations or some combination of them.  No lonely answers.</p>
</paragraphs>

<!--<paragraphs>
	<title>Projects: 5% of semester grade<title>
		<p>Once or twice a quarter, larger and often more open ended projects will be assigned in addition to the problem sets.  All the problem-set rules except the ``Set'' and ``Pick'' options apply.<p>
</paragraphs>-->

<paragraphs>
  <title>Tests: 44% of semester grade</title>
  <p>Tests are closed-book.  Calculators are allowed. If you are asked to show <q>by hand</q> how to do integration or differentiation then you must show the process for obtaining an answer as if you had no calculator (though you may use your calculator to check).  Equations sheets <em>may</em> be provided.  Tests are typically 70-80 minutes long and consist of open-ended questions in which you will show how you arrive at your answers.  There will be eight tests over the course of the year.</p>
		<p>You may ask questions or clarifications during the test/exam.  Whether or not they will be answered will depend on Dr. Horner's judgement of whether or not doing so would enhance or undermine the integrity of the test/exam.  No resources other than those mentioned at the top of each test or exam are permissible during that test or exam.</p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Semester Exams: 20%</title>
  <p>Semester exams are in the same format and given under the same conditions as tests except that they are 2 hours long with a 15 minute grace period allowed (the grace period is <em>not</em> increased for students with extended time by school policy).</p>
  <p>All students will take a Winter Exam.  Seniors with a class average and a fourth-quarter average of 85% or better may elect to skip the spring exam. In which case, all the 20% will be redistributed proportionally among the other grading categories.</p> 
</paragraphs>  
			
<paragraphs>
<title>Test Corrections and Reflections: 4% of semester grade</title>
		<p>As a separate component you will be required to submit \textbf{test reflections} for each test and the winter exam.  This will consist of 
		<ul>
			<li>photos of your graded test,</li>
			<li>reflection on what went wrong on each problem or problem part marked off, </li>
			<li>a corrected solution </li>
			<li>plus if that answer materially changes further parts of the problem, corrections of them as well, and</li>
			<li>an overall reflection of what went well and what went poorly in your preparation for and taking of the text.</li>
		</ul></p> 
		
		<p>The intent is both that tests be learning experiences and to replace incorrect practice with correct practice.</p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>What to do if you get stuck</title>
	If you get stuck doing homework, feel uncertain of your grasp of something, or feel completely confused, you have a variety of possible next steps.
	<p><ul>
		<li>Imagine asking someone the question you want to ask (Mr. Twyford's ``ask the rubber duck'' method).  Sometimes this helps you at least see more clearly why you're stuck.</li>
		<li>Reread your notes, the textbook, or review any simulations, videos, etc. suggested for that unit.</li>
		<li>Discuss your question with a classmate.  (Note that this is NOT the same as asking what the answer is or even asking what calculation to do.)</li>
		<li>Consult your teacher.  There's usually ``Extra Help / Office Hours'' twice a week during Community Time.  Lunch, before school, or right after school may also work.  Mutual free bells also work, though an email checking no conflicts have arisen is useful.</li>
		<li>Turn your attention to something else (possibly including sleeping if it's night) on your to-do list and come back later with a refreshed brain.</li>
	</ul></p>
</paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
  <title>Absence from class</title>
	<p>See the <q>Student Life Guide</q>, <q>Condensed Student Policies</q>, and <q>Planned Absence Form</q> in the Canvas Student Central course for how to handle absences.</p>
</paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
  <title>Accommodations</title>
	<p>If you have accommodations, especially testing accommodations, please speak with Dr. Horner briefly so we have the same understanding of how they will play out in the context of this particular class.  Generally speaking for extended time, take half during class and the other half in Study Hall during the first available free bell.</p>
</paragraphs>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-scale">
  <title>Grading Scale</title>
	<p>Final letter grades will be computed according to the table of percentages below.</p>
	
	<tabular>
    <col halign="right"/> <col halign="left"/> <col halign="right"/> <col halign="left"/> 
    <row><cell>A+</cell><cell>97 <m>\lte</m> grade</cell> <cell>C+</cell><cell>77 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 80</cell></row>
    <row><cell>A</cell><cell>93 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 97</cell> <cell>C</cell><cell>73 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 77</cell></row>
    <row><cell>A-</cell><cell>90 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 93</cell> <cell>C-</cell><cell>70 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 73</cell></row>
    <row><cell>B+</cell><cell>87 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 90</cell> <cell>D+</cell><cell>67 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 70</cell></row>
    <row><cell>B</cell><cell>83 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 87</cell> <cell>D</cell><cell>63 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 67</cell></row>
    <row><cell>B-</cell><cell>80 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 83</cell> <cell>D-</cell><cell>60 <m>\lte</m> grade <m>\lt</m> 63</cell></row>
	</tabular>
	<p>Grades less than 60% will be reported as NP = not passing.</p>
	
	<p>Percentages will be rounded to the nearest whole number and exact half points will round up in the final assignment of grades.  But note that Canvas will be set not to show rounded grades.</p>
</subsection>

</section>
